WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.356
Lesson four point four.

2
00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:04.447
State-space models: the maths under S4D,

3
00:00:04.447 --> 00:00:06.107
built up from a leaky bucket.

4
00:00:10.063 --> 00:00:12.244
Start with a bucket of water with a small

5
00:00:12.244 --> 00:00:13.230
hole in the bottom.

6
00:00:13.542 --> 00:00:16.034
Water pours in from a tap: call the inflow

7
00:00:16.034 --> 00:00:17.280
u, in litres a second.

8
00:00:17.591 --> 00:00:19.616
Water leaks out of the hole, and the

9
00:00:19.616 --> 00:00:21.952
fuller the bucket, the harder it pushes,

10
00:00:21.952 --> 00:00:23.665
so the leak grows with the level.

11
00:00:23.977 --> 00:00:26.209
Here, at any moment, it leaks half the

12
00:00:26.209 --> 00:00:28.494
level's number in litres a second: two

13
00:00:28.494 --> 00:00:30.363
litres in, one litre a second out.

14
00:00:30.674 --> 00:00:32.854
The level, call it x, is the bucket's

15
00:00:32.854 --> 00:00:35.295
state: one number that sums up everything

16
00:00:35.295 --> 00:00:37.475
poured in so far, with the recent pours

17
00:00:37.475 --> 00:00:38.202
counting most.

18
00:00:38.633 --> 00:00:39.783
Write it as an equation.

19
00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:42.322
On the left, x prime of t: how fast the

20
00:00:42.322 --> 00:00:44.383
level is changing right now, in litres a

21
00:00:44.383 --> 00:00:44.718
second.

22
00:00:45.006 --> 00:00:46.587
The leak: a times the level.

23
00:00:46.874 --> 00:00:48.168
The inflow: b times u.

24
00:00:48.456 --> 00:00:50.468
Our bucket has a equal to minus a half,

25
00:00:50.468 --> 00:00:52.481
the minus sign because the leak drains,

26
00:00:52.481 --> 00:00:54.637
and b equal to one: all the tap's water

27
00:00:54.637 --> 00:00:55.595
lands in the bucket.

28
00:00:56.083 --> 00:00:57.175
Check it with a number.

29
00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:59.690
Two litres in the bucket and the tap off:

30
00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:01.731
x prime is minus a half times two, minus

31
00:01:01.731 --> 00:01:01.921
one.

32
00:01:02.205 --> 00:01:03.914
The level is falling at one litre a

33
00:01:03.914 --> 00:01:04.246
second.

34
00:01:04.531 --> 00:01:06.762
And time here runs smoothly: t can be any

35
00:01:06.762 --> 00:01:08.755
number of seconds, not only whole steps.

36
00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:11.270
Our drums live on slots; turning one into

37
00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:12.979
the other is lesson four point five.

38
00:01:16.353 --> 00:01:18.720
Turn the tap off and let the bucket drain

39
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:19.622
from two litres.

40
00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:22.553
The level follows a curve: x of t equals

41
00:01:22.553 --> 00:01:24.469
two times e to the minus a half t.

42
00:01:24.807 --> 00:01:26.949
After one second, one point two one

43
00:01:26.949 --> 00:01:27.343
litres.

44
00:01:27.681 --> 00:01:30.105
After two seconds, zero point seven four.

45
00:01:30.443 --> 00:01:32.415
After four, zero point two seven.

46
00:01:32.897 --> 00:01:33.156
Why e?

47
00:01:33.414 --> 00:01:35.183
The level's slope is always minus a half

48
00:01:35.183 --> 00:01:37.039
times the level itself, and e to the a t

49
00:01:37.039 --> 00:01:38.592
is the curve whose slope is a times

50
00:01:38.592 --> 00:01:38.894
itself.

51
00:01:39.153 --> 00:01:41.051
Look at two seconds: the level is zero

52
00:01:41.051 --> 00:01:42.864
point seven four, which is thirty-seven

53
00:01:42.864 --> 00:01:43.511
percent of two.

54
00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:45.625
Every two seconds it falls to thirty-seven

55
00:01:45.625 --> 00:01:47.351
percent, one over e, of what it was.

56
00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:49.594
Two seconds is one over a half: the time

57
00:01:49.594 --> 00:01:49.983
constant.

58
00:01:50.242 --> 00:01:52.097
Lesson four point three used the same word

59
00:01:52.097 --> 00:01:52.658
for the LSTM.

60
00:01:53.119 --> 00:01:55.172
Leave the tap running at one litre a

61
00:01:55.172 --> 00:01:57.002
second instead, starting empty.

62
00:01:57.335 --> 00:01:59.553
The level climbs until the leak matches

63
00:01:59.553 --> 00:02:02.105
the inflow: half of x equals one, so x

64
00:02:02.105 --> 00:02:02.937
settles at two.

65
00:02:03.594 --> 00:02:05.937
Now picture a cup poured in for every note

66
00:02:05.937 --> 00:02:07.027
the guitarist plays.

67
00:02:07.354 --> 00:02:09.424
The level tells you how much has been

68
00:02:09.424 --> 00:02:11.712
played recently, and the last couple of

69
00:02:11.712 --> 00:02:12.748
seconds count most.

70
00:02:13.075 --> 00:02:15.200
That is the kind of memory this lesson

71
00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:15.581
builds.

72
00:02:15.908 --> 00:02:18.251
S4D's real inputs are not notes, as four

73
00:02:18.251 --> 00:02:19.177
point five shows.

74
00:02:22.507 --> 00:02:23.946
One number holds one time scale.

75
00:02:24.216 --> 00:02:25.836
Take two buckets under the same tap.

76
00:02:26.106 --> 00:02:28.355
One with a big hole: a equals minus two, a

77
00:02:28.355 --> 00:02:29.750
time constant of half a second.

78
00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:31.999
One with a tiny hole: a equals minus a

79
00:02:31.999 --> 00:02:33.799
tenth, a time constant of ten seconds.

80
00:02:34.069 --> 00:02:35.823
After one second the fast one has kept

81
00:02:35.823 --> 00:02:37.623
fourteen percent, the slow one ninety

82
00:02:37.623 --> 00:02:37.983
percent.

83
00:02:38.252 --> 00:02:40.277
One remembers the last beat, the other the

84
00:02:40.277 --> 00:02:40.907
last few bars.

85
00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:43.556
Stack the two levels into a column and

86
00:02:43.556 --> 00:02:45.234
write both equations at once.

87
00:02:45.581 --> 00:02:47.895
The leak rates sit on the diagonal of a

88
00:02:47.895 --> 00:02:49.978
square table of numbers, a matrix.

89
00:02:50.325 --> 00:02:52.524
The column of ones says the tap feeds

90
00:02:52.524 --> 00:02:52.813
both.

91
00:02:53.889 --> 00:02:55.870
In general: x prime equals A x plus B u.

92
00:02:56.134 --> 00:02:58.026
x is now N numbers, a column, N by one.

93
00:02:58.290 --> 00:02:59.699
A is the state matrix, N by N.

94
00:02:59.963 --> 00:03:01.679
On its diagonal, how fast each state

95
00:03:01.679 --> 00:03:03.703
leaks; off the diagonal, how one state

96
00:03:03.703 --> 00:03:04.540
pours into another.

97
00:03:04.804 --> 00:03:05.684
Here those are zero.

98
00:03:05.948 --> 00:03:08.104
B is the input matrix, N by one: how much

99
00:03:08.104 --> 00:03:09.601
of the input goes into each state.

100
00:03:09.865 --> 00:03:11.801
And u, the input, is one number at each

101
00:03:11.801 --> 00:03:12.725
moment, one by one.

102
00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:17.330
The state is kept inside.

103
00:03:17.639 --> 00:03:18.929
To use it, read it out.

104
00:03:19.239 --> 00:03:21.354
y, the output, is one number at each

105
00:03:21.354 --> 00:03:21.715
moment.

106
00:03:22.025 --> 00:03:24.449
C, the output matrix, one by N, says how

107
00:03:24.449 --> 00:03:25.842
much of each state to read.

108
00:03:26.152 --> 00:03:28.525
D is a skip: a single number that passes

109
00:03:28.525 --> 00:03:30.640
the input straight through to the output.

110
00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:33.517
For the two buckets: read the total, C

111
00:03:33.517 --> 00:03:35.891
equals one, one, and no skip, D equals

112
00:03:35.891 --> 00:03:36.155
zero.

113
00:03:36.472 --> 00:03:37.843
y is the two levels added.

114
00:03:38.322 --> 00:03:40.449
That's the whole thing: a state-space

115
00:03:40.449 --> 00:03:40.745
model.

116
00:03:41.042 --> 00:03:43.218
A state that sums up the past, a rule for

117
00:03:43.218 --> 00:03:45.395
how it changes, A and B, and a rule for

118
00:03:45.395 --> 00:03:46.681
reading it out, C and D.

119
00:03:46.977 --> 00:03:49.351
It is linear: every term is a fixed number

120
00:03:49.351 --> 00:03:50.687
times a state or the input.

121
00:03:50.983 --> 00:03:53.061
Nothing is squashed, as the LSTM's tanh

122
00:03:53.061 --> 00:03:55.187
squashes, and nothing is switched on and

123
00:03:55.187 --> 00:03:55.385
off.

124
00:03:56.237 --> 00:03:57.764
One name to keep straight.

125
00:03:58.117 --> 00:04:00.350
Textbooks call the state x, as here.

126
00:04:00.703 --> 00:04:03.229
In our code, x is the slot's five inputs.

127
00:04:03.582 --> 00:04:06.050
The code calls the S4D state s, so from

128
00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:08.224
four point five on, the state is s.

129
00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:13.733
Now a different system: a mass on a

130
00:04:13.733 --> 00:04:16.093
spring, with a damper, like a car's shock

131
00:04:16.093 --> 00:04:16.555
absorber.

132
00:04:16.863 --> 00:04:19.274
Pull it and let go: it bounces, and the

133
00:04:19.274 --> 00:04:20.146
bounces die away.

134
00:04:20.454 --> 00:04:23.122
It needs two states: where the mass is, p,

135
00:04:23.122 --> 00:04:24.712
and how fast it is moving, v.

136
00:04:25.169 --> 00:04:27.258
Its rules: position changes by the

137
00:04:27.258 --> 00:04:27.728
velocity.

138
00:04:28.041 --> 00:04:30.077
Velocity changes by the spring pulling

139
00:04:30.077 --> 00:04:32.531
back, minus ten point one two times p, the

140
00:04:32.531 --> 00:04:34.829
damper braking, minus v, and any push u.

141
00:04:35.142 --> 00:04:37.074
As a matrix, A has numbers off the

142
00:04:37.074 --> 00:04:39.319
diagonal: the two states push on each

143
00:04:39.319 --> 00:04:41.616
other, so neither can be worked out alone.

144
00:04:42.377 --> 00:04:44.719
Every matrix like this has a few rates of

145
00:04:44.719 --> 00:04:47.061
its own, which say how the whole system

146
00:04:47.061 --> 00:04:47.507
behaves.

147
00:04:47.841 --> 00:04:49.960
For this spring they are not ordinary

148
00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:52.525
numbers but complex numbers: points on a

149
00:04:52.525 --> 00:04:54.867
plane, so many across and so many up,

150
00:04:54.867 --> 00:04:57.153
written a plus b i, where i times i is

151
00:04:57.153 --> 00:04:57.711
minus one.

152
00:04:58.046 --> 00:05:00.443
This A's two rates are minus a half plus i

153
00:05:00.443 --> 00:05:02.339
pi, and minus a half minus i pi.

154
00:05:02.674 --> 00:05:05.239
The across part, minus a half, is a decay

155
00:05:05.239 --> 00:05:06.967
rate, like the bucket's leak.

156
00:05:07.302 --> 00:05:10.146
The up part, pi, is a frequency: how fast

157
00:05:10.146 --> 00:05:12.711
it swings, in radians a second, where one

158
00:05:12.711 --> 00:05:14.272
full turn is two pi radians.

159
00:05:15.131 --> 00:05:16.250
Here is the useful part.

160
00:05:16.529 --> 00:05:18.254
Measure the system along its own two

161
00:05:18.254 --> 00:05:19.886
directions instead of position and

162
00:05:19.886 --> 00:05:21.890
velocity, and A becomes diagonal, with

163
00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:23.382
those two rates on the diagonal.

164
00:05:23.662 --> 00:05:25.713
Two states again, each on its own, each

165
00:05:25.713 --> 00:05:27.624
obeying x prime equals a x, now with a

166
00:05:27.624 --> 00:05:28.091
complex a.

167
00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:30.422
The two are mirror images, a plus b i and

168
00:05:30.422 --> 00:05:32.519
a minus b i, which is why S4D keeps one of

169
00:05:32.519 --> 00:05:34.291
each pair, as four point five shows.

170
00:05:37.563 --> 00:05:39.866
Take one of those states alone: x prime

171
00:05:39.866 --> 00:05:42.065
equals a x, with a equal to minus a half

172
00:05:42.065 --> 00:05:42.577
plus i pi.

173
00:05:42.884 --> 00:05:45.186
The answer has the bucket's shape: e to

174
00:05:45.186 --> 00:05:45.595
the a t.

175
00:05:45.902 --> 00:05:47.079
Split the power in two.

176
00:05:47.386 --> 00:05:49.433
e to the minus a half t is the bucket's

177
00:05:49.433 --> 00:05:50.916
decay: it sets the size.

178
00:05:51.223 --> 00:05:53.576
e to the i pi t is a turn: a point going

179
00:05:53.576 --> 00:05:55.776
round a circle, half a turn every second.

180
00:05:56.187 --> 00:05:58.138
Together: a point that turns while it

181
00:05:58.138 --> 00:05:58.500
shrinks.

182
00:05:58.772 --> 00:05:59.181
A spiral.

183
00:05:59.453 --> 00:06:01.221
At half a second it has made a quarter

184
00:06:01.221 --> 00:06:03.398
turn: straight up, size zero point seven

185
00:06:03.398 --> 00:06:03.671
eight.

186
00:06:03.943 --> 00:06:06.029
At one second, half a turn: minus zero

187
00:06:06.029 --> 00:06:07.344
point six one, on the left.

188
00:06:07.616 --> 00:06:09.884
At two seconds, one full turn: back on the

189
00:06:09.884 --> 00:06:11.471
right, at zero point three seven.

190
00:06:11.743 --> 00:06:13.421
The same thirty-seven percent as the

191
00:06:13.421 --> 00:06:14.555
bucket after two seconds.

192
00:06:14.958 --> 00:06:17.341
Now watch only the across part, the real

193
00:06:17.341 --> 00:06:18.671
part, as time goes on.

194
00:06:19.003 --> 00:06:21.497
It swings back and forth and dies away:

195
00:06:21.497 --> 00:06:22.605
the spring's bounce.

196
00:06:22.938 --> 00:06:25.155
The size shrinks at the decay rate, a

197
00:06:25.155 --> 00:06:27.759
half; it swings once every two seconds,

198
00:06:27.759 --> 00:06:29.256
set by the frequency, pi.

199
00:06:30.523 --> 00:06:31.630
This is not only a spring.

200
00:06:31.886 --> 00:06:33.631
S4D starts each of its states at exactly

201
00:06:33.631 --> 00:06:35.632
this kind of rate: minus a half plus i pi

202
00:06:35.632 --> 00:06:37.122
n, for n from zero to thirty-one.

203
00:06:37.378 --> 00:06:39.166
n equals zero has no turn: it is the

204
00:06:39.166 --> 00:06:39.464
bucket.

205
00:06:39.719 --> 00:06:41.422
n equals one is the spiral you just saw.

206
00:06:41.678 --> 00:06:43.423
Higher n turn faster, up to thirty-one

207
00:06:43.423 --> 00:06:44.019
times as fast.

208
00:06:44.275 --> 00:06:46.105
How far round the spiral one slot of music

209
00:06:46.105 --> 00:06:48.106
goes is set by a step size, and that, with

210
00:06:48.106 --> 00:06:49.980
where training moved these rates, is four

211
00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:50.448
point five.

212
00:06:53.797 --> 00:06:55.672
So why build memory from something this

213
00:06:55.672 --> 00:06:56.000
simple?

214
00:06:56.282 --> 00:06:57.782
Because the forgetting is fixed.

215
00:06:58.064 --> 00:07:00.174
Each state shrinks at its own rate, set by

216
00:07:00.174 --> 00:07:02.377
A, whatever comes in, and nothing squashes

217
00:07:02.377 --> 00:07:04.394
it, so a slow state really does keep its

218
00:07:04.394 --> 00:07:05.566
contents for a long time.

219
00:07:05.847 --> 00:07:07.817
And because the output is just a weighted

220
00:07:07.817 --> 00:07:09.739
sum of past inputs, which is what lets

221
00:07:09.739 --> 00:07:11.521
four point five compute every slot at

222
00:07:11.521 --> 00:07:11.755
once.

223
00:07:12.112 --> 00:07:14.195
But linear alone can't make decisions.

224
00:07:14.524 --> 00:07:16.662
Lesson three point one showed that two

225
00:07:16.662 --> 00:07:19.020
linear layers in a row are just one linear

226
00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:19.349
layer.

227
00:07:19.678 --> 00:07:21.706
The same holds here: a stack of

228
00:07:21.706 --> 00:07:23.899
state-space models with nothing between

229
00:07:23.899 --> 00:07:25.873
them would be one state-space model.

230
00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:27.656
So S4D never stacks them bare.

231
00:07:27.919 --> 00:07:29.717
Each block runs the state-space layer,

232
00:07:29.717 --> 00:07:31.516
then a GELU, the smooth bend from four

233
00:07:31.516 --> 00:07:33.490
point seven point ten, then a linear layer

234
00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:35.332
with a gate called a GLU, which is four

235
00:07:35.332 --> 00:07:37.043
point six's to explain, and adds the

236
00:07:37.043 --> 00:07:37.701
result back on.

237
00:07:37.964 --> 00:07:39.981
One way to read it: the linear part does

238
00:07:39.981 --> 00:07:41.999
the remembering; the GELU's bend and the

239
00:07:41.999 --> 00:07:43.841
GLU's gate decide what to do with what is

240
00:07:43.841 --> 00:07:44.324
remembered.

241
00:07:44.587 --> 00:07:45.947
Four of these blocks make S128.

242
00:07:47.329 --> 00:07:50.005
Say it in one breath: a state-space model

243
00:07:50.005 --> 00:07:52.568
keeps a state that leaks at fixed rates, x

244
00:07:52.568 --> 00:07:54.959
prime equals A x plus B u, and reads it

245
00:07:54.959 --> 00:07:56.610
out, y equals C x plus D u.

246
00:07:56.952 --> 00:07:59.059
A complex rate is a decay rate and a

247
00:07:59.059 --> 00:08:00.824
frequency together, a spiral.

248
00:08:01.166 --> 00:08:03.671
The memory is linear, and the deciding is

249
00:08:03.671 --> 00:08:05.891
left to the nonlinear blocks around it.

250
00:08:06.233 --> 00:08:08.454
This pairs with section one of the SSM

251
00:08:08.454 --> 00:08:10.731
research page from the twenty-fourth of

252
00:08:10.731 --> 00:08:13.237
September, what an SSM is, next to your

253
00:08:13.237 --> 00:08:13.521
LSTM.

254
00:08:13.863 --> 00:08:16.710
Next, four point five: S4D, from seconds

255
00:08:16.710 --> 00:08:19.272
to slots, and two ways to compute the same

256
00:08:19.272 --> 00:08:19.728
numbers.

